<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pekah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 740–732 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pekah of Israel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pekah was king of Israel from about 740 to 732 BCE, an army officer who seized the throne by killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekahiah">Pekahiah</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a>, he tried to force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> into an anti-Assyrian coalition, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> invaded, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a> and deported part of the population. Pekah was then killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshea">Hoshea</a>, who submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pekah.jpg" alt="Pekah" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pekah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
